<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lat Dior</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1842–1886</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Lat Dior Diop, Lat Joor</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lat Dior Diop was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damel">Damel</a>, or king, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolof">Wolof</a> kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayor">Cayor</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>, ruling at intervals between 1862 and 1886. He became a Muslim and resisted French expansion, above all the railway from Dakar to Saint-Louis that crossed his territory. Deposed by the French, he was killed in battle at Dekhelé in 1886 and is honoured as a national hero in Senegal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lat-dior.jpg" alt="Lat Dior" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ji-Elle · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BelAirMurals6.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
